      "text": "To solve the expression \\(1 - 4 + 7 - 10 + 13 - \\cdots - 46 + 49 - 52 + 55\\), we first need to identify the pattern in the sequence. The sequence alternates between positive and negative terms, and the absolute values progress by 3 each time.\n\nWe can group the terms in pairs to simplify the calculation:\n\\[\n(1 - 4) + (7 - 10) + (13 - 16) + \\cdots + (49 - 52) + 55\n\\]\nEach pair \\( (1 - 4), (7 - 10), (13 - 16), \\ldots, (49 - 52) \\) sums to \\(-3\\). The sequence of absolute values is \\(1, 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25, 28, 31, 34, 37, 40, 43, 46, 49, 52, 55\\), which are the terms of an arithmetic sequence with the first term 1 and common difference 3, up to 55.\n\nTo find the number of terms in this sequence, we use the formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence:\n\\[\na_n = a + (n-1)d\n\\]\nwhere \\(a = 1\\), \\(d = 3\\), and \\(a_n = 55\\). Solving for \\(n\\):\n\\[\n55 = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 3 \\implies 55 = 1 + 3n - 3 \\implies 55 = 3n - 2 \\implies 57 = 3n \\implies n = 19\n\\]\nSo, there are 19 terms in the sequence. Since the terms are grouped into pairs of 2, there are \\(19/2 = 9.5\\), but since we are dealing with pairs, we have 9 pairs and one remaining term, which is 55. Therefore, the number of pairs is 9.\n\nThe sum of each pair is \\(-3\\), so the sum of 9 pairs is:\n\\[\n9 \\times (-3) = -27\n\\]\nAdding the remaining term 55, we get:\n\\[\n-27 + 55 = 28\n\\]\nThus, the value of the expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{28}\n\\]",
